BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khoj-mozilla-counterstructures-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Khoj x Mozilla Foundation: Counterstructures Residency 20
 26 (Delhi)
DESCRIPTION:Counterstructures is Mozilla Foundation's cultural R&D residenc
 y\, in which creative technologists prototype alternatives to dominant tec
 h systems: cultural artifacts\, installations and tools that question and 
 reframe them. Each edition operationalises the findings of a regional part
 icipatory assembly into thematic tracks\; the Delhi edition\, hosted by Kh
 oj International Artists' Association\, works from Mozilla's assemblies wi
 th G5A in Delhi and Mumbai and runs on two tracks. RETAINING CULTURAL AUTH
 ORITY asks what happens when oral\, embodied or community-held knowledge i
 s converted into datasets and machine-readable forms\, which cultural mate
 rials become visible to AI systems and which do not\, and how communities 
 can retain authority over what is digitised\, interpreted\, reused or deli
 berately kept private. REMEMBERING AND THINKING WITH MACHINES asks where t
 hought resides when people think with machines\, at what stage human judgm
 ent enters the system\, and what cultural or professional knowledge is los
 t when judgment is standardised through automation. Proposals may respond 
 to one or both tracks and can take many forms\, but must critically counte
 r the questions the tracks raise rather than simply illustrate them. WHAT 
 RESIDENTS DO: prototype an artifact\, installation\, system\, artwork or t
 ool for public exhibition\; take part in listening\, reading\, workshops a
 nd facilitated critique\, testing ideas with peers and community members\;
  write a 750-word report on the project's development and insights for the
  Counterstructures report and online archive\; and present the work public
 ly. WHO IT IS FOR: creative technologists and multidisciplinary artists us
 ing technology as a medium for cultural inquiry\, bringing critical perspe
 ctives and research-driven practice. Experience with AI can be early-stage
  or advanced\, but SOME PROFICIENCY WITH CODING IS REQUIRED. ELIGIBILITY: 
 this Delhi edition is open to Indian nationals only. Earlier editions ran 
 with tiat in San Francisco (responding to Hollywood's 8 Rules for AI) and 
 with Rhizome in New York\, whose residents showed at the New Museum. APPLY
  through the Mozilla Foundation form at https://mozillafoundation.tfaforms
 .net/f/khojresidency\; the guiding principles for the call are set out in 
 the form. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Khoj International 
 Artists' Association in collaboration with Mozilla Foundation\nLocation: N
 ew Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIONALS ONLY
 .\nAward: Mozilla's Creative Futures: Counterstructures programme publishe
 s USD 2\,500 per resident (USD 2\,000 stipend plus a USD 500 materials bud
 get)\, along with weekly in-person work sessions\, guest lectures from pra
 ctitioners across art\, technology and community design\, and publication 
 of the finished project in the collective documentation report. CAVEAT: ne
 ither the Khoj call page nor the Mozilla application form restates a figur
 e for this six-week Delhi edition\, and the published package covers the t
 en-week format\, so confirm the amount with Khoj before applying. No appli
 cation fee.\nDuration: Six weeks\, 1 October to 12 November 2026\, running
  weekly in Delhi\, with final projects presented publicly 13 to 15 Novembe
 r 2026.\n\nMore: https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstru
 ctures-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstructures-residen
 cy/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIO
 NALS ONLY.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:algorave-istanbul-open-call-artists@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Algorave Istanbul: Open Call for New Artists and Performe
 rs
DESCRIPTION:UNPAID COMMUNITY CALL. Algorave Istanbul\, the local node of th
 e international algorave scene (live-coded music and visuals performed for
  an audience)\, is looking for new artists to create\, experiment and perf
 orm with. The call names live coding\, sound\, visuals\, audiovisual perfo
 rmance\, and generative or reactive systems\, and says explicitly that it 
 is open to formats the organisers have not thought of yet. WHO CAN APPLY: 
 artists based in Istanbul. There is no stated experience requirement and n
 o selection process described. Applications close 30 September 2026. WHAT 
 IS OFFERED: performance opportunities and collaboration within the collect
 ive. No fee\, stipend or expenses are mentioned anywhere in the call\, so 
 treat this as unpaid unless the organisers say otherwise. APPLY through th
 e Google Form\, or email algoraveistanbul@gmail.com. The call is published
  in Turkish and English.\n\nOrganisation: Algorave Istanbul\nLocation: Ist
 anbul\, Turkiye. Applicants must be based in Istanbul.\nAward: UNPAID\, an
 d no figure of any kind is published. The call states no fee\, honorarium\
 , stipend or expenses\; it is an invitation to join a local live-coding co
 mmunity and perform with it. Listed here as a deliberate exception to the 
 desk's paid-only rule because it is one of very few live-coding openings i
 n the region. No application fee.\nDuration: Open-ended involvement with t
 he collective\; no fixed term or number of performances stated.\n\nMore: h
 ttps://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdboafDes-9bslmHhaHwO3jpEN4YgS1bxe
 3R3wUlYfzvBlNsA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdboafDes-9bslmHhaHwO3jpEN4YgS
 1bxe3R3wUlYfzvBlNsA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Istanbul\, Turkiye. Applicants must be based in Istanbul.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sat-research-creation-residency-arts-technologies-2026-28@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261018
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:Deadline: SAT: Research-Creation Residency Program in Arts and Tech
 nologies 2026-28 (Montreal)
DESCRIPTION:The Societe des arts technologiques (SAT) in Montreal runs a re
 search-creation residency program in arts and technologies for artists and
  collectives working in the performing arts and/or media arts. The residen
 cy funds a research-creation stage of a project in technological arts rath
 er than the finalisation of a finished work\, so the call weighs the explo
 ration process and the innovation in it above a polished outcome. Each sel
 ected project receives a CAD 10\,000 grant\, whether the applicant is an i
 ndividual artist or a collective\, plus access to SAT spaces and equipment
 \, technical and creative support from the SAT teams\, mentoring and train
 ing activities\, and documentation and archiving. Access to spaces and equ
 ipment is assessed against the project's needs and granted subject to avai
 lability. Travel\, meal and accommodation costs beyond the grant are not c
 overed. Open to professional artists and artistic collectives worldwide\, 
 at any level of experience and from any primary discipline\, provided the 
 profile and project suit immersive technologies\; collectives must submit 
 a joint portfolio showing they can carry out the project independently\, p
 articularly the visual and sound creation inside a dome. Each application 
 must include a form and supporting documents setting out the concept\, the
  technical solutions considered\, and what will be explored during the res
 idency. One application per project and per person\; incomplete applicatio
 ns are not evaluated\; use permanent\, downloadable links rather than WeTr
 ansfer or similar temporary services. Accommodations and alternative suppo
 rt documents (large print and so on) are available on request at residence
 s@sat.qc.ca. Deadline 18 October 2026. Apply through the Google Form linke
 d from the SAT open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: Societe des arts technolog
 iques (SAT)\nLocation: Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Sa
 int-Laurent\, Montreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and col
 lectives from anywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.\n
 Award: CAD 10\,000 grant per selected project\, for individual artists and
  collectives alike. Also included: access to SAT workspaces during the res
 idency periods\, access to equipment and software resources subject to ava
 ilability\, mentoring and training activities (on site and remote)\, creat
 ive and technical support from the SAT teams\, and documentation and archi
 ving of the work. Travel\, meals and accommodation beyond the grant are NO
 T covered. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, extendable up to th
 ree months depending on the project\; residencies run across the 2026-28 p
 rogram.\n\nMore: https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-pr
 ogram-in-arts-technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-program-in-arts-
 technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
LOCATION:Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Saint-Laurent\, M
 ontreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and collectives from a
 nywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sphera-media-lab-2026-open-call-discourse@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sphera Media Lab 2026: Open Call for Masterclasses and Ke
 ynotes
DESCRIPTION:European Lab and the Sphera Network are opening submissions for
  the discourse programme of the next Sphera Media Lab\, the annual forum f
 or people defending independent media in Europe. THEMES: technological ind
 ependence (sovereignty\, tools\, mutualisation)\; economic independence (c
 ooperative models)\; editorial independence (press freedom\, resisting the
  far right). FORMATS SOUGHT: masterclasses\, led by an expert sharing adva
 nced knowledge\, technique and hands-on insight\; and keynotes\, inspiring
  talks grounded in a specific case study. WHO CAN PROPOSE: media outlets\,
  associations\, researchers and professionals. The organisers state they p
 ay particular attention to parity and diversity across their programmes an
 d ask that proposals take those criteria into account. WHAT IS COVERED: se
 lected contributors travel to Brussels with transport\, accommodation and 
 a fee covered. THE EVENT: Sphera Media Lab 2026 runs on 3 December 2026 at
  LaVallee in Brussels\, a full day of conferences\, masterclasses and work
 shops for independent media professionals from across Europe\, alongside t
 he premiere of a documentary on threats facing European journalists. APPLI
 CATION: submit your masterclass or keynote proposal via the Typeform at ar
 ty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM before 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: 
 European Lab (Arty Farty) and the Sphera Network\, coordinated by Medianes
 \nLocation: LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. 
 Proposals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should
  reflect a European perspective.\nAward: Selected contributors receive a f
 ee plus travel to Brussels and accommodation\, all covered by the organise
 rs. The fee amount is not published in the call. No submission fee.\nDurat
 ion: One-day event on 3 December 2026\; contributors deliver a single mast
 erclass or keynote\n\nMore: https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. Prop
 osals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should ref
 lect a European perspective.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iclc-2027-antwerp-call-for-participation@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: ICLC 2027: International Conference on Live Coding\, Call
  for Participation (Dialogues)
DESCRIPTION:The International Conference on Live Coding is dedicated to pra
 ctices and research focused on technologies and philosophies that interpre
 t the use of computer code and the public writing of algorithms as perform
 ative gestures. The 10th edition runs 9 to 13 March 2027 in Antwerp\, host
 ed by Lambdasonic (TOPLAP Belgium) with the Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp
  and De Singel. THEME: 'Dialogues'\, framing live coding as continuous imp
 rovisational exchange between coder\, machine and audience\, and across di
 sciplines. Suggested topics include dialogues between live coding and acou
 stic musicians\, dancers\, theatre\, classically trained musicians reading
  live-coded (or graphical) scores\, band and jazz-combo settings\, live-co
 der ensembles\, ten-minute live-coded mini-operas\, DMX lighting systems\,
  graphics and shader live coding\, robotics (pen plotters\, textile-weavin
 g robots)\, design-as-performance\, community building\, diversity/equity/
 inclusion\, accessibility of live coding languages and interfaces\, educat
 ion\, and algorave. The venues will facilitate collaborations with residen
 t dancers\, a jazz combo (back-line provided: drums\, guitar amp\, bass am
 p\, piano)\, a string quartet reading scores a prima vista\, visual artist
 s\, students and researchers where possible. FORMATS: short papers (2-4 pa
 ges) and long papers (5-12 pages) using the provided template\; live perfo
 rmances (800-word abstract\, max 150-word programme note\, technical rider
 )\; workshops (short 2h or long 4h\, max 700-word abstract\, held in Engli
 sh or Dutch)\; installations (max 700-word abstract\, technical questionna
 ire plus a measured plan or sketch\, with the interactive aspect clearly d
 efined)\; and satellite events. All contributors submit a max 150-word bio
 graphy. Peer review is single-blind with at least two reviewers per submis
 sion. LANGUAGE: submissions must be in English\, except that live performa
 nce\, installation and workshop proposals may also be submitted in another
  language (workshops: English\, Dutch or French) with a machine-translated
  English version\, stated as such. Substantial use of generative AI in wri
 ting papers and performance abstracts must be disclosed\; proofreading and
  translation are exempt. In-person presentation is encouraged\, with remot
 e proposals considered and sessions live-streamed where possible\; the org
 anisers ask attendees to minimise travel impact and to link up with nearby
  satellite events to turn a long trip into a mini-tour. The call notes att
 ention to access needs\, care responsibilities and other barriers to parti
 cipation. SATELLITE EVENTS: live coding nodes\, art schools and other orga
 nisations worldwide are invited to host their own events before or after I
 CLC 2027\; submit a declaration of interest via Fourwaves before 1 Februar
 y 2027 with a principal contact\, city and any confirmed details\, and joi
 n #iclc-satellite-events on the TOPLAP Discord. Hosts are asked to seek lo
 cal funding for accommodation\, travel and artist fees\; the committee can
 not fund or organise these but will coordinate dates and publish your even
 t. TO APPLY: submit via Fourwaves at https://event.fourwaves.com/iclc2027/
 pages using the templates at https://codeberg.org/iclc/iclc-templates (rea
 d the README for full proposal instructions). Submission deadline extended
  to 1 September 2026\; acceptance notification 1 November 2026. CALL FOR R
 EVIEWERS: anyone active in a live-coding-related field can volunteer by em
 ailing a short description and keywords of their expertise to iclc@lambdas
 onic.be\, the same address for general questions.\n\nOrganisation: Lambdas
 onic (TOPLAP Belgium)\, Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel\nLoca
 tion: Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel International Arts Cent
 er\, Antwerp\, Belgium. Open to anyone engaged with live coding\; remote p
 articipation considered.\nAward: NO submission fee. The call does not stat
 e artist fees\, travel support or accommodation for accepted contributors\
 , so budget for self-funded attendance unless the organisers announce othe
 rwise. Proceedings are published with a DOI per article. Satellite-event h
 osts can request a Letter of Intent to support their own local funding app
 lications.\nDuration: Five-day conference\, 9 to 13 March 2027. Workshops 
 are 2 hours (short) or 4 hours (long).\n\nMore: https://iclc.toplap.org/20
 27/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://iclc.toplap.org/2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
LOCATION:Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel International Arts Ce
 nter\, Antwerp\, Belgium. Open to anyone engaged with live coding\; remote
  participation considered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-heritage-innovation-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260918
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Heritage Innovation (September 2026 round
 )
DESCRIPTION:Heritage Innovation funds projects and research tracks that con
 tribute to substantive innovation in the heritage sector. Examples given b
 y the fund include developing new narratives about cultural heritage\, str
 engthening regional collaboration across the sector\, redesigning how a co
 llection is presented\, and feasibility studies on technological applicati
 ons such as AI or gamification\, which makes this one of the more tech-adj
 acent Mondriaan schemes. WHO CAN APPLY: museums and collection-holding cul
 tural heritage institutions\, including archives\, in the Netherlands and 
 the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. Cultural organisations in the heritage 
 sector may also apply if they collaborate with one or more collection-hold
 ing institutions. All applicants must be non-profit\, not-for-profit entit
 ies. NOT FUNDED: fixed costs\, restoration\, building museums\, or regular
  operations. CONDITIONS: institutions must subscribe to the Fair Practice 
 Code\, the Culture Governance Code and the Diversity and Inclusion Code\, 
 and must pay visual artists fairly where artists are involved. AMOUNTS: pr
 ojects get up to 40% of variable project-related costs on a minimum projec
 t budget of EUR 25\,000\; research tracks get up to 70% of costs capped at
  EUR 15\,000 with no minimum budget. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund onli
 ne system after registering an account. Deadline 17 September 2026 before 
 16:00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time.\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\n
 Location: Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Neth
 erlands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.\nAward: Projects: up to 40%
  of variable project-related costs\, with a minimum project budget of EUR 
 25\,000. Research tracks: up to 70% of costs\, capped at EUR 15\,000\, wit
 h no minimum budget. No application fee.\nDuration: Project or research tr
 ack. This card covers the 17 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www
 .mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-innovation/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-inno
 vation/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Nether
 lands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:video-pool-matr-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T204153Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Video Pool: Media Art + Technology Residency (MATR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:Video Pool's Media Art + Technology Residency supports experime
 ntal\, independent\, technology-based work. The selected artist or artists
  develop a new project (or adapt and transform an existing one) in direct 
 response to the intimate scale and specific character of the Poolside Gall
 ery\, culminating in an exhibition there. The call is deliberately open on
  medium: video\, sound\, installation\, digital media\, performance\, inte
 ractive technologies\, expanded cinema and interdisciplinary practices are
  all welcome. VP asks proposals to speak clearly to your practice\, the qu
 estions\, materials or ideas that drive the work\, and how that work might
  grow\, shift or take new form inside the gallery. It is explicitly intere
 sted in expansive proposals\, work that pushes past the gallery's four wal
 ls to engage its surrounding spaces (hallways\, windows\, projection wall)
  or that reimagines what the gallery itself can hold\, and in projects tha
 t engage technology as a creative\, critical\, aesthetic or socio-politica
 l tool. Priority is given to artists from equity-seeking communities whose
  practices contribute to the advancement of experimental media arts. Resid
 ents get full access to VP's available equipment\, facilities and technica
 l resources throughout the residency\, and applicants are encouraged to re
 view the equipment and facilities list before proposing. ELIGIBILITY: open
  to VP members (this is a member submission call)\; no nationality restric
 tion is stated\, but the residency and exhibition are in person in Winnipe
 g. APPLICATION: artist bio (1\,500 characters)\, headshot\, description of
  your artistic practice (3\,000 characters)\, project proposal for MATR (3
 \,000 characters)\, equipment and facility needs (750 characters)\, descri
 ption of how you would use the space (750 characters)\, CV\, up to five im
 ages (optional) and up to two video or documentation links. Submit via the
  online form at videopool.org/matr-2027-application/ by 30 August 2026\; a
 lternative submission formats can be arranged by contacting Emma (emma@vid
 eopool.org) or Iyunade (iyunade@videopool.org). General enquiries: info@vi
 deopool.org\, 204-949-9134.\n\nOrganisation: Video Pool Media Arts Centre\
 nLocation: In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gall
 ery\, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.\nAward
 : CAD 2\,250 artist fee\, covering both the residency period and the subse
 quent Poolside Gallery exhibition. Residents also get full access to VP's 
 equipment\, facilities and technical resources\; the Poolside Gallery itse
 lf is equipped with two projectors and four speakers. Applicants must be V
 P members\, so a membership is purchased separately\, but there is no sepa
 rate application fee.\nDuration: Up to two months to develop and install t
 he project\, including one month of dedicated access to the Poolside Galle
 ry. Residency takes place in Winter 2027\; exhibition dates TBD.\n\nMore: 
 https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-applic
